# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, Windows
# included.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. GitHub's Windows runners check out with core.autocrlf
# set, which rewrites every text file to CRLF. Two things in this
# repository break when that happens, and the first one did:
#
#   - yamllint's new-lines rule requires \n and fails the workflow files,
#     so `scripts/ci.sh` exits non-zero before it ever configures a build.
#     That is what the Windows leg's first real run reported, on
#     2026-09-04: `1:68 [new-lines] wrong new line character: expected \n`.
#   - the shell scripts are run by Git Bash. A CRLF script fails in ways
#     that read as anything but a line-ending problem, because the \r
#     becomes part of the last argument on the line.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere,
# so both hazards go away for every file at once rather than per
# extension. The binary rules below stop that normalisation touching
# files where a byte is a byte.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Content this project reads but never writes. Unreal package formats are
# binary; a normalisation pass through one would corrupt it. Nothing under
# content/ is committed (docs/design.md rule 15), so these are here to be
# correct rather than because a match is expected.
*.unr binary
*.utx binary
*.uax binary
*.umx binary
*.u   binary
*.utab binary

# Baked and captured artefacts.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.mp4 binary
*.ico binary
